  24	/**
  25	 * Answers whether this account can legally queue a unit right now, and which one.
  26	 *
  27	 * Roles are taken in the order a miner's opening takes them: cargo first, because every later
  28	 * fleet action needs somewhere to put loot and resources, then the colony ship that unlocks a
  29	 * second planet, which is what fleetsave, transport and scouting all wait on. The two intel-driven
  30	 * roles come after the opening, because each waits on an observation: defence when the host says a
  31	 * hostile is inbound or when the planet has something the need evaluator says is worth a wall, and
  32	 * a combat escort when a fresh report shows a defended target the account's own fleet is not
  33	 * expected to crack.
  34	 *
  35	 * Power is the one role the building queue shares: a planet that is short and whose capacity the
  36	 * building queue cannot take buys it from the yard instead, which is what a player does when the
  37	 * plant is out of reach or the queue is busy. It is offered only when the building planner's own
  38	 * gate says no, so the two never compete over the same shortfall.
  39	 *
  40	 * Which object fills each role is read from the host: cargo is the ship with the largest cargo
  41	 * capacity per metal-equivalent cost the planet can actually queue, combat is the hull with the best
  42	 * attack per metal-equivalent cost, the colony ship is the ship the host's own colonisation mission
  43	 * consumes, and defence is the component `DefenseCompositionPlanner` says this account's doctrine is
  44	 * most behind on. A mod-added hull with a better ratio becomes its role's unit with no edit here.
  45	 *
  46	 * The standing wall pass below is the one place the account's whole planet list is weighed at once,
  47	 * and it is the only role that is collected before it is chosen: the account's wall spreads across
  48	 * its planets one login at a time, each login serving the first planet still standing at zero.
  49	 *
  50	 * A bare planet whose yard cannot yet take any defence unit is reached by the building planner's
  51	 * defence-prerequisite step, not here: a wall waits on the shipyard that builds it. A planet that
  52	 * holds an order in the yard is served, whether or not the host has finished building it.
  53	 *
  54	 * The pass's plan is marked, because where the order is placed decides whether it happens: the
  55	 * session's building steps are executed first and spend the balance the wall was priced against, so
  56	 * the host refused the wall order and the planet stayed bare on every login (QUAL-003). The marker
  57	 * lets the schedule run the login's first wall ahead of those steps.
  58	 *
  59	 * A bare sibling whose yard cannot yet take a defence unit is the building planner's business: the
  60	 * wall pass that planner runs first stands the yard the unit waits on, so a login that finds a
  61	 * sibling naked spends itself on that yard rather than on the account's habits, and the wall reaches
  62	 * every sibling rather than only the ones whose yard already exists. The pass below speaks for that
  63	 * sibling even when it is not the planet whose balance pays: a wall is placed on the planet that
  64	 * stands at zero, whatever the login chose. A sibling whose yard is ready
  65	 * keeps its own stock for the wall order because a bare planet that spends its balance every login
  66	 * never reaches the price of the unit the wall order waits on.
  67	 */
