I'll assert the planner for these (energyGap/shortfall) and the situation story for the behaviour. To build the planet service in the test: `array_values(app(PlayerServiceFactory::class)->make($this->currentUserId, true)->planets->all())[0]` (as AmbitionReachTest does).

Now — I also need `ships('solar_satellite', N)` for the "past it" case to give free energy. With no solar plant... satellites give energy: 10,000 sats × 30 = 300k. Hmm, `ships()` adds units to the current planet; energy stats need a refresh: `$planet->updateResourceProductionStats(false)`? The planners refresh themselves. In my planner assertions I should refresh: `$planet->updateResources(false); $planet->updateResourceProductionStats(false);` like verify-cohorts does. Hmm, does `energy()` depend on the stored energy_max column updated by that call? Yes. So call those two before asserting.

OK. And AmbitionReachTest's third test fix: change the fixture so the energy-priced technology is not yet owned. Let me write it as: keep `foreach (getResearchObjects() as $research) playerSetResearchLevel(20)`, then after building `$planet`, set the energy-priced ones to 0... but the test computes `$energyPriced` from the catalogue *after* the levels; if I set them to 0 the filter still finds graviton (price energy > 0 ∧ requirements met (lab 12)) → ✔ and then energyGap > 0 ✔ after my fix (level 0 < ceiling 1) ✔. And `shortfall > 0` ✔.

